#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4381bf05-59cf-5164-8798-58e814b9a555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715192} {:answer/id #uuid "44b932b2-e799-51ab-a86f-4b45c2bbb328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{160}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715194} {:answer/id #uuid "8c486199-ab33-59d6-b937-52aafe291c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715202} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8549ba-08ac-5bce-a636-8bcc1e9cd00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715209} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb0ae7-438e-5308-94bc-1fe2bd60c123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715194} :answer-field/id #uuid "210210f9-bf3b-5f42-ab47-3e86e71a12da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a636888-8d28-5c5e-be75-f374d8a412fd" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{y}$ along the line segment from $(4,0)$ to $(4,8)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(4,0)$ to $(4,8).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(4)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(4)\\,i+(8)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(4)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(8)\\,j) \\\\  & =(4)\\,i+(8 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=4$ and $y=8 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(8 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(8)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(8)^2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4+\\sqrt{2(8 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =4 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (4 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 4)\\cdot 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =32\\int_0^1 (\\sqrt{t} + 1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =32[\\frac{2 \\cdot t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + t]_0^1 \\\\  & =32(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "391c0889-2862-5b7c-9848-932bdc7d0d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17 \\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715189} {:answer/id #uuid "4f470f52-d7f0-596d-a1b0-b59b23a040dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17 \\sqrt{5}}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715197} {:answer/id #uuid "60d4bc76-4114-5a67-9f7b-daec47a60eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715198} {:answer/id #uuid "756a07a5-5361-53a6-8748-4ad443b8ad61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{85}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715199} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7d9913-1024-53dd-891b-a289ce32636b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715189} :answer-field/id #uuid "36b1d832-b690-50d3-981c-3110350e7197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b872693-83a3-59ed-b613-9f324c1dd7b1" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x y$ along the line segment from $(-1,-2)$ to $(-3,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,-2)$ to $(-3,-1).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(-1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j$ and $b=(-3)\\,i+(-1)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+t((-2)\\,i+(1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 2 \\cdot t - 1)\\,i+(t - 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=- 2 \\cdot t - 1$ and $y=t - 2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t - 1)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t - 2)j \\\\  & =(-2)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x \\cdot y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1(- 2 \\cdot t - 1)(t - 2) \\\\  & =- 2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t + 2)\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t + 2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[- \\frac{2 \\cdot t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\cdot t^{2}}{2} + 2 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+(2))-\\sqrt{5}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{17 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d42980-dc2d-56ad-94ee-c3459c6dd64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715183} {:answer/id #uuid "43e50f2c-180e-5fb1-8a49-32c2fe0fd7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2 e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715193} {:answer/id #uuid "4d571f1a-f650-5ad8-bd0e-8e09630b3057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{5}{2 e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715196} {:answer/id #uuid "8c62e71d-629d-5d96-be4b-faba71e39535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715203} {:answer/id #uuid "927cd613-0339-58bf-aa75-cf2980519053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{2 e^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715196} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2746b9-6697-53fa-966a-fc6e7780e3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "313f7369-bfdb-5e4e-8ed5-75fd5386bc7a" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=e^{2 x - y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,-4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,-4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(-3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((-3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 \\cdot t)\\,i+(- 4 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=- 3 \\cdot t$ and $y=- 4 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 \\cdot t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 4 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(-3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-4)^2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=e^{2 \\cdot x - y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =e^{2(- 3 \\cdot t)+-1(- 4 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =e^{- 2 \\cdot t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponent has derivative $-2$. With $u=- 2 t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $0$ and $-2$. Write $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (e^{- 2 \\cdot t})\\cdot 5\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =5\\int_0^1 (e^{- 2 \\cdot t})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =5[- \\frac{e^{- 2 \\cdot t}}{2}]_0^1 \\\\  & =5\\left(- \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot e^{2}}\\right)-5\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{5}{2 \\cdot e^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ef17db-85a9-5e05-b565-20c46491936b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13} \\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{4 e^{4}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715184} {:answer/id #uuid "355d6ca3-cc94-5cfc-9355-c3dbfdad4439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4} - \\frac{13}{4 e^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715187} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1c9f2f-c604-5ada-be8e-91fe2c6d8be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4 e^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715190} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2fba77-177c-58f9-b8f7-529e681541fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715195} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7f3fe1-e0de-51fd-939b-1479d864ddda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{e^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715184} :answer-field/id #uuid "f32734d2-670b-5bdf-adf1-29a4d1cae4d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd4b390e-42be-5720-a814-8bcc9ccd72fe" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x - 2 y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(2,3)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(2,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(2)\\,i+(3)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((2)\\,i+(3)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2 \\cdot t)\\,i+(3 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2 \\cdot t$ and $y=3 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\cdot t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(3)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+(3)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=e^{x - 2 \\cdot y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =e^{1(2 \\cdot t)+-2(3 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =e^{- 4 \\cdot t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponent has derivative $-4$. With $u=- 4 t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=-4\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $0$ and $-4$. Write $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (e^{- 4 \\cdot t})\\cdot \\sqrt{13}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\int_0^1 (e^{- 4 \\cdot t})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}[- \\frac{e^{- 4 \\cdot t}}{4}]_0^1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot e^{4}}\\right)-\\sqrt{13}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot e^{4}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5aaa62-558c-5a75-9cb5-f75979c4c39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{208}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715200} {:answer/id #uuid "8efde685-6cbd-510d-ae84-0d36c2db30d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715204} {:answer/id #uuid "ad22a76b-a84f-5174-a5a1-e1231a897ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715207} {:answer/id #uuid "cb748e28-4aba-5b38-a72b-bb64aa17dea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715210} {:answer/id #uuid "ffae357d-b834-58d9-b422-b7efa0fdd422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715204} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ecd51a7-f19f-548f-affc-c76063080a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dcb2c7fc-28da-5916-824c-451d7e631fef" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{y}$ along the line segment from $(3,0)$ to $(3,4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,0)$ to $(3,4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(3)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(3)\\,i+(4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(4)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(4 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3$ and $y=4 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(4)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(4)^2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3+\\sqrt{1(4 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =2 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (2 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 3)\\cdot 4\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =12\\int_0^1 (\\frac{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{t}}{3} + 1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =12[\\frac{4 \\cdot t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9} + t]_0^1 \\\\  & =12(1+\\frac{4}{9})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02212492-84c9-5ca5-87fc-b9126f8bf67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715217} {:answer/id #uuid "0240adf1-d98e-5639-a547-9778311adc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715218} {:answer/id #uuid "10f6dbf3-88fc-51cf-bd91-e3d9b7fd0fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715222} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5ac6f9-fbe3-5dea-9972-68e628acd93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715225} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8a690d-fc74-53a3-b7b8-d138cb67bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715222} :answer-field/id #uuid "0accaa08-d2eb-52fd-bde3-a7abcb3b6864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b0362b3-6733-5b71-ab93-2749d058686d" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=- x - 2 y$ along the line segment from $(3,-3)$ to $(3,-5)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-3)$ to $(3,-5).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j$ and $b=(3)\\,i+(-5)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3$ and $y=- 2 \\cdot t - 3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=- x - 2 \\cdot y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =-1(3)+-2(- 2 \\cdot t - 3) \\\\  & =4 \\cdot t + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (4 \\cdot t + 3)\\cdot 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2\\int_0^1 (4 \\cdot t + 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2[2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =2((2)+(3))-2(0) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b80203c-685c-5a1e-b897-1fa87501c7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715226} {:answer/id #uuid "6289a6ca-6511-5b05-ae2a-caf27ac5ea05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715227} {:answer/id #uuid "8048f743-6442-59aa-8c9f-ea5601501169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715228} {:answer/id #uuid "d48bcf97-48bf-5252-b219-76d977b573f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715240} {:answer/id #uuid "de7bfaa0-2a27-5be3-83e5-00384db4e9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715226} :answer-field/id #uuid "28f354c6-b7ce-56f5-9011-f77c48f163f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4880ae9-7fc2-53a8-a527-433cbb52e65a" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=- x - 2 y$ along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(0,4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(0,4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(2)\\,j$ and $b=(0)\\,i+(4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(2)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(2 \\cdot t + 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=0$ and $y=2 \\cdot t + 2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(0)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\cdot t + 2)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(2)^2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=- x - 2 \\cdot y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =-1(0)+-2(2 \\cdot t + 2) \\\\  & =- 4 \\cdot t - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (- 4 \\cdot t - 4)\\cdot 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2\\int_0^1 (- 4 \\cdot t - 4)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2[- 2 \\cdot t^{2} - 4 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =2((-2)+(-4))-2(0) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d859a45-2d24-5733-b5ce-090bb96eee20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715221} {:answer/id #uuid "af5f8014-3716-5633-b445-db2ab9fb5a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715234} {:answer/id #uuid "b517e76d-315f-58aa-bbe6-31bca347a6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715235} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdda10f-ccff-52ae-9ba8-bdeb80ab358a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715241} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b1bda7-79a0-5976-b7c6-0d1f831b4aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715235} :answer-field/id #uuid "855c1db7-705c-5165-be38-ad94479bd374" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c3fadd4b-2de1-5903-9803-1d2eb7373efc" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x - y$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(3,3)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(3)\\,j$ and $b=(3)\\,i+(3)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(3)\\,j+t((3)\\,i+(0)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3 \\cdot t)\\,i+(3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3 \\cdot t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\cdot t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x - y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1(3 \\cdot t)+-1(3) \\\\  & =3 \\cdot t - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (3 \\cdot t - 3)\\cdot 3\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =3\\int_0^1 (3 \\cdot t - 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =3[\\frac{3 \\cdot t^{2}}{2} - 3 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =3(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+(-3))-3(0) \\\\  & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06199236-da89-5271-948a-0022e1a533e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715219} {:answer/id #uuid "242e5a50-8379-5f2d-9137-3a87cb276a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715223} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ee6599-2f66-517d-b0bb-7da07d2e42a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715233} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e7b11c-71de-58b3-8624-17d36a179e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715236} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6068d3-e609-5161-ae4e-f53436497056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715219} :answer-field/id #uuid "996577c3-8db5-5abc-8743-7d13a25613cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea03a794-7163-5c16-ab0b-3d87b83ae8c9" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + y$ along the line segment from $(0,-3)$ to $(0,-5)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,-3)$ to $(0,-5).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(-3)\\,j$ and $b=(0)\\,i+(-5)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=0$ and $y=- 2 \\cdot t - 3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(0)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x + y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1(0)+1(- 2 \\cdot t - 3) \\\\  & =- 2 \\cdot t - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\cdot 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2[- t^{2} - 3 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =2((-1)+(-3))-2(0) \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d87ee9-ca18-53d9-8e3b-876c5cc847b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2} \\left(e^{3} + e^{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715245} {:answer/id #uuid "135a6512-9ee6-51f6-8f8a-299c51b7014e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715246} {:answer/id #uuid "52b281c8-da09-50af-8748-383ff503c1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2} e^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715250} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9b2622-7d89-598d-821c-7283cdc4bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2} \\left(- e^{3} + e^{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715255} {:answer/id #uuid "cf38dc0c-ea4c-5ed7-871d-f6224dd09b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{2} e^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715255} :answer-field/id #uuid "6108313b-97c3-5120-9d35-19ac1cf6d9bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25f6a5e5-dd8b-5f3c-a389-af80c07d95ec" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x y}$ along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(2,4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(2,4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(1)\\,i+(3)\\,j$ and $b=(2)\\,i+(4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(3)\\,j+t((1)\\,i+(1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t + 1)\\,i+(t + 3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=t + 1$ and $y=t + 3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 1)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 3)j \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\left(2 \\cdot x + 2\\right) \\cdot e^{x \\cdot y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2(t + 1)+2)e^{(t + 1)(t + 3)} \\\\  & =(2 \\cdot t + 4)e^{t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 ((2 \\cdot t + 4)e^{t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3})\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\int_0^1 ((2 \\cdot t + 4)e^{t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3})\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3,$ $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 \\cdot t + 4)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\sqrt{2}\\int_{3}^{8}e^u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}[e^u]_{3}^{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(e^{8}-e^{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\left(- e^{3} + e^{8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eae9537-53e2-5329-a725-2b37a8456fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715248} {:answer/id #uuid "50d2d4bd-6772-5d13-9299-0adc80baada1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5} \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715249} {:answer/id #uuid "578fb4fd-b946-550e-a1bb-57d3468590af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{5 \\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715251} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5ab190-3845-5322-a0ef-f24db46b7228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715253} {:answer/id #uuid "f7825599-5599-5ac3-ba42-a32d53dec4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715258} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6247103-1410-507e-bfa7-65baf8bb5c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186715254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d6a9db83-3d82-56b0-9ed1-9f225cbf7226" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sec^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(- x + y\\right)}{12} \\right)}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t)\\,i+(- 2 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=t$ and $y=- 2 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sec^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot \\left(- x + y\\right)}{12} \\right)}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sec^2\\left(\\frac{\\pi((- 2 \\cdot t)-(t))}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sec^2\\left(- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (\\sec^2\\left(- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4}\\right))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^1 (\\sec^2\\left(- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4}\\right))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4},$ $\\mathrm{d}u=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\sec^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}[\\tan u]_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}(\\tan\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\tan(0)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d7bcb3-fa3a-5b40-8ff8-64393f6b428a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^2t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big)\\sqrt{4(t-1)^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716096} {:answer/id #uuid "457c3318-0f14-5878-bb9f-efc04506faa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^3t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big)\\sqrt{4t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716104} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1f8875-9132-5b5b-826d-b12d350d72a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^3t(t^2-2)\\sqrt{4(t-1)^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716107} {:answer/id #uuid "84eee1b6-87e4-5ba1-95ec-d6f6d42146f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^3t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big)\\sqrt{4(t-1)^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716109} {:answer/id #uuid "a370f941-5e81-54c6-af5c-74b3a6136b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^3t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716109} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5450a4-c87f-5656-914a-027565358a4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2490fe06-be9e-5064-be58-542e4652fa6d" :question/problem "![The parabola from (-1,0) through its vertex (-2,1) to (2,3), with traversal arrows.](images/00/00384c711760782c3d4b5d7801ee1292116fd53679d365affb98e921365fac32.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $(y-1)^2=x+2$ from $(-1,0)$ to $(2,3)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate." :question/worked-solution "The equation is simplest as a function of $y$:\n\n$$\nx=(y-1)^2-2.\n$$\n\nSet $y=t$. This follows the whole arc, including its vertex, with one parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle (t-1)^2-2,t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter bounds are the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints, not their $x$-coordinates. Differentiate to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2(t-1),1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{4(t-1)^2+1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into $f(x,y)=xy$ gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_Cxy\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^3 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^3 t\\big((t-1)^2-2\\big)\\sqrt{4(t-1)^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $2(t-1)$ changes sign at the vertex, but its square in the speed keeps $\\mathrm{d}s$ nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b89f7b-33a4-5b92-8c31-616fd2b69fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716100} {:answer/id #uuid "57330ebc-c7d9-5fc4-b828-8f69cb881cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716106} {:answer/id #uuid "c840de34-6991-56fc-bb0d-9267a46ebf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716113} {:answer/id #uuid "cfee3fa9-a8fe-5592-a1b1-826130081ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716114} {:answer/id #uuid "e8742aa5-3f3e-5ad8-9ed1-8dceb10f4f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716116} :answer-field/id #uuid "396879ea-8e7b-5666-a2ab-f01f64996449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bcce86b-e5b6-5c99-97a6-af655d1fa44c" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C (3x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^2\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x-2y=1$ from $(1,0)$ to $(5,2)$. Which expression is missing under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t$.*" :question/worked-solution "Solve the curve equation for $x$ and set $y=t$:\n\n$$\nx=1+2y,\\qquad \\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1+2t,t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThe bounds are the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints. The arc-length factor is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle 2,1\\rangle,\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=3(1+2t)-t=3+5t.\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(3x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^2 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^2(3+5t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $3+5t$; the factor $\\sqrt{5}$ is already outside the integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0987c549-6766-5ac5-994d-99912942d288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\int_1^6\\frac{\\sqrt{t^2+9}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716097} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec61e01-1f00-51f1-bb8b-ecb0c87c5ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\int_{1/2}^3\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716099} {:answer/id #uuid "338908a2-d1b5-5236-a165-d8e2a7d29d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\int_1^6\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716101} {:answer/id #uuid "83e65219-0804-5a51-9a13-d23f29802082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\int_1^6\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+1}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716108} {:answer/id #uuid "db45a30c-155a-512e-8f9e-b9b0578eed7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\int_1^6\\sqrt{t^4+9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716101} :answer-field/id #uuid "a325ac89-a9b4-5efc-a722-1d61624b3887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac2d128e-d2f2-5e3f-95b7-a0d9a6ef61b9" :question/problem "![The reciprocal curve from (3,1) to (1/2,6), with arrows showing traversal.](images/ee/ee8e5ae8e5ae5ea03a9c4c8208db9b44ba978621d318719bdc546f0b62493bc4.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C 2xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of $x=\\dfrac{3}{y}$ from $(3,1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac12,6\\right)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t$.*" :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$ in $x=3/y$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{t},t\\right\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 6.\n$$\n\nUse the $y$-coordinates, $1$ and $6$, for the bounds. Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\left\\langle-\\frac{3}{t^2},1\\right\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^4}+1}\n=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge1$, the denominator $t^2$ is positive. Substituting into the scalar function gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)t^2=6t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C2xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_1^6 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_1^6 6t\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=6\\int_1^6\\frac{\\sqrt{t^4+9}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative $x$-component of the velocity is squared in the arc-length factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09bb0a83-be31-54c1-b3c5-9abc699a0df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(125-5\\sqrt5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716117} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1b064c-ada2-5118-a3c9-22c0d40cc46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716123} {:answer/id #uuid "726a5d42-7b37-58e1-b961-8bb51fd6bf33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716124} {:answer/id #uuid "7be38820-5c3e-53ab-878b-214b98368042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125-5\\sqrt5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716127} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d0945e-76c9-53c0-b686-43c8df3c9d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125-5\\sqrt5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716127} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71f4512-5d37-52d4-9659-2595eb3ae37e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b86fdc6-60e2-50ae-bff8-ac286685ac71" :question/problem "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C\\frac{6x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x=y^2$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt6)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$, so $x=t^2$. The parameter bounds come from the endpoints' $y$-coordinates:\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le\\sqrt6.\n$$\nCompute the derivative and its magnitude:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle2t,1\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{4t^2+1}.\n$$\nSince $t\\ge1$, the denominator is nonzero and\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=\\frac{6t^2}{t}=6t.\n$$\nThus the scalar line integral is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\frac{6x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_1^{\\sqrt6}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=6\\int_1^{\\sqrt6}t\\sqrt{4t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=4t^2+1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=8t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. When $t=1$, $u=5$; when $t=\\sqrt6$, $u=25$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6\\int_1^{\\sqrt6}t\\sqrt{4t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n&=\\frac34\\int_5^{25}u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\frac34\\left[\\frac23u^{3/2}\\right]_5^{25}\\\\\n&=\\frac12\\left(25^{3/2}-5^{3/2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{125-5\\sqrt5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e928268-1e59-5d53-81be-95cb2386fb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716119} {:answer/id #uuid "7381b092-0afc-5d20-8d35-d580d7878a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716125} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e05d61-1cc1-5ac1-9d82-ad7a898e131e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716130} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bc9b78-cfe0-5e36-ac3f-5857deb57e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716131} {:answer/id #uuid "dea82377-df0a-5919-8b6c-10f64a2c2cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716119} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a3f6c0e-3170-503b-88a0-d0d3a20042f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ee4cfec-c4ed-5486-8389-76f5741ede3f" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 2y\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The $y$-coordinates of the endpoints give $2\\le t\\le3$, so\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2t-4,t\\rangle,\\qquad 2\\le t\\le3.\n$$\nDifferentiate and compute the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle2,1\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\\sqrt5.\n$$\nThe scalar function becomes $f(\\mathbf r(t))=2t$. Therefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C 2y\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_2^3 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\int_2^3 2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5\\left[t^2\\right]_2^3\\\\\n&=\\sqrt5(9-4)\\\\\n&=5\\sqrt5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381861c0-c74d-51a2-b346-b32ab46a57a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(7\\sqrt7-5\\sqrt5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716120} {:answer/id #uuid "401e22e1-e4d0-5dba-8042-58093257c646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt7-5\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716122} {:answer/id #uuid "7750b1a9-bc94-51e4-8fdb-4e1c8ee81ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716126} {:answer/id #uuid "a12b91a5-c4c3-5b78-9ed3-65ae5087228c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16-4\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716129} {:answer/id #uuid "cf47a759-71dd-5d69-995a-a1c662fe9630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716122} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68c9824-bbad-57a3-a928-93395d298672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fb02f139-6159-512b-a44e-08d2e968d4d4" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 3x^6e^{-2y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=e^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt3,\\ln3)$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. Substituting into the curve gives\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle e^{t/2},t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le\\ln3.\n$$\nThe parameter bounds are the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints. The derivative requires a chain-rule factor of $\\tfrac12$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\left\\langle\\frac12e^{t/2},1\\right\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\frac14e^t+1}\n=\\frac12\\sqrt{e^t+4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function and combine the exponentials:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=3(e^{t/2})^6e^{-2t}\\\\\n&=3e^{3t}e^{-2t}\\\\\n&=3e^t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C3x^6e^{-2y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^{\\ln3}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\frac32\\int_0^{\\ln3}e^t\\sqrt{e^t+4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=e^t+4$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=e^t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The bounds become $u=5$ and $u=7$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac32\\int_0^{\\ln3}e^t\\sqrt{e^t+4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n&=\\frac32\\int_5^7u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\frac32\\left[\\frac23u^{3/2}\\right]_5^7\\\\\n&=7^{3/2}-5^{3/2}\\\\\n&=7\\sqrt7-5\\sqrt5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8763a4f3-5638-567d-a2f3-e163d1dee379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=9\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716429} {:answer/id #uuid "acda2e98-071b-541e-8376-a4d49e1b0b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716431} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ee287d-3f2e-5fdc-8c34-3e138e8ab61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716432} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2dc69a-ea85-56be-adae-7057c61baf0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716435} {:answer/id #uuid "ee264cbe-7194-5e6c-923b-4329deda1585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716436} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c71d85d-5353-5644-969f-13fddf86af70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9362760-76f9-5618-8e3a-00c8d6194326" :question/problem "![](images/da/da44dc52332fab5cee40203fd6d0979cfa86bdc6d166c96372ba4e77b12476d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The intercepts are $(\\pm4,0)$ and $(0,\\pm3)$, so the horizontal radius is $4$ and the vertical radius is $3$. Each radius is the distance from the center to the ellipse, not the full axis length.\n\nAn ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=4$ and $b=3$, so\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe cosine and sine each range from $-1$ to $1$, giving exactly the horizontal and vertical extents shown."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d99f4b4-6efb-5922-9e1a-de5ce63a0649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=6\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716421} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2d2d8d-4e56-5ae4-95eb-ed2b85457c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716423} {:answer/id #uuid "8181f919-f430-5c89-9cc5-5d6b4c8e8d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=36\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716427} {:answer/id #uuid "81b0dd2e-51cb-5c81-9793-87a5090c35cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=4\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716428} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e36fa0-f179-51a8-9641-fd42ec628e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716433} :answer-field/id #uuid "77cf7136-d3f8-5c27-a16e-dbc362054e94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eeb94fea-2b34-5d2d-9e43-e7c4d51d102e" :question/problem "![](images/76/767fd967e905084a50f0a5d4ab1e2c9ad3e8e9bf62777a608100c418d8863a2a.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $6$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $A(4,0)$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=4$ and $b=6$, so\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, these equations give $(4,0)$. As $\\theta$ increases from $0$ to $\\pi/2$, the point moves from $(4,0)$ to $(0,6)$ along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse. This is counterclockwise. The remaining quarter-turns pass through $(-4,0)$ and $(0,-6)$; the interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal.\n\nThe pair $x=4\\sin\\theta,\\ y=6\\cos\\theta$ traces the same ellipse, but starts at $(0,6)$ and moves clockwise. Matching only the shape is insufficient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61921863-97e4-55f5-a533-ea649e4bb758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716424}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716424} :answer-field/id #uuid "1091fe0f-0b38-5e9c-8137-c51269880541" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716420} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4a3a49-bab2-54f7-98de-9594bf988084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716430} :answer-field/id #uuid "4682faf4-cac5-56ac-96c8-26f57e1d1eaf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fc44a1a4-6b9f-566f-8cfd-5fa0d5db5d02" :question/problem "![](images/12/12cbe2b704e91e62b6405f46868b226a1fbcf56e17da9b0927c798639c7795f5.png)\n\nThe ellipse shown is centered at the origin. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse it once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the marked point $A$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "Read the radii from the graph: the horizontal intercepts are $(\\pm3,0)$ and the vertical intercepts are $(0,\\pm2)$. Thus the horizontal radius is $3$ and the vertical radius is $2$.\n\nAn ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=3$ and $b=2$, so\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe marked point is $A(3,0)$. At $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, so the equations start at $A$. For increasing $\\theta$, the point travels through\n\n$$\n(3,0)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (0,2)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (-3,0)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (0,-2)\n$$\n\nat parameter values $0$, $\\pi/2$, $\\pi$, and $3\\pi/2$, respectively. This order is counterclockwise. As $\\theta$ approaches $2\\pi$, the point returns toward $A$, completing one traversal without including the starting point a second time."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716459 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e4bb66-4d7b-5e15-b647-47baba300ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716465} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed1ac21-2247-5fa6-b4ab-cdaf3ddaef4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "100a8ac9-ba77-5e20-9199-62c2e252b789" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+2,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-1,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$\n\nEnter only the left-hand side of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Undo each translation before dividing by the trigonometric coefficient. From the $x$-equation,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\theta+2\\\\\nx-2&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{x-2}{3}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $y$-equation,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\theta-1\\\\\ny+1&=4\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{y+1}{4}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}=1,\n$$\n\nso enter $\\frac{(x-2)^2}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{16}.$ The center is $(2,-1)$ and the horizontal and vertical radii are $3$ and $4.$ The full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "418007eb-1f7a-583b-9981-cbb1f9d68b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{3}+\\frac{y^2}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716462} {:answer/id #uuid "4983511a-088b-5cf7-b4c8-9f8a74b6f698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716463} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e12929-0abc-53de-9742-19e359945c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{9}-\\frac{y^2}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716471} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e193e3-980a-5215-95bf-49506ea41842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716472} {:answer/id #uuid "e66e6a0f-b960-59a6-b3fc-1a26e23f4aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{9}+y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716463} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9857153-a3fc-5369-9499-f5a46ee897e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d136b30-1ba7-5903-baa2-2d1a427cf00c" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe trigonometric terms have been isolated for you:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}=\\cos\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{y}{2}=\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSquare both equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$ Choose the resulting Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Square each isolated equation, including its denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{x^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta,\\\\\n\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{y^2}{4}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these equations and apply the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe parameter interval covers a full turn, so this equation describes the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cebbf37-b0c0-5501-9588-1097e92636b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{3}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716460} {:answer/id #uuid "400c8922-7fc6-5bfe-a66d-1d6e56d3ddd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{9}-\\frac{y^2}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716461} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6e1e72-2eb9-5864-8ada-d973c3a37bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716464} {:answer/id #uuid "99d66815-239c-56fc-9a71-5b10b6c52811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716469} {:answer/id #uuid "ba178a0a-2e9f-50bf-8220-853a9d81b77b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{16}-\\frac{y^2}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be2188c-40d7-57a5-8087-0d472a5b2235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3687686-0ad8-55fb-9612-1aa8cffb1aeb" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate $\\theta$ using the Pythagorean identity. First isolate the trigonometric terms and square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{x}{3}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{x^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{y}{4}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{y^2}{16}&=\\sin^2\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding and using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nThe full interval $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$ traces the whole ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4557fb75-abfe-5d5d-bba7-1afee952c458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716478} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca36873-97db-55cc-8be4-98a3282741de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta+2,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716480} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff70676-6e5f-527b-9060-6ba0b7e3d371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta-2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716481} {:answer/id #uuid "65a511af-a7f0-5d5d-8705-44cbdf2b340b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16\\cos\\theta+2,\\quad y=9\\sin\\theta-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716482} {:answer/id #uuid "695bc344-d04e-50ff-8742-a3b00139b9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta+2,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716478} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf57dca8-3b7b-5f07-9613-de85e7a4240c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "17de7748-8533-5c36-8524-23a1c7191289" :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes, with dashed horizontal and vertical lines through its center.](images/fb/fbf33b01d4b8dd517d4ce08d5d65bfd66560a208efe27fa88999cb267c0a433c.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations, defined for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$, generates the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes on the graph are $x=-2$ and $x=6$, and the vertical extremes are $y=-4$ and $y=2$. Their midpoints give the center:\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{-2+6}{2}=2,\\qquad k=\\frac{-4+2}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal and vertical radii are half the corresponding full widths:\n\n$$\na=\\frac{6-(-2)}{2}=4,\\qquad b=\\frac{2-(-4)}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSubstituting gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\qquad y=3\\sin\\theta-1,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThese equations reproduce all four extreme coordinates on the graph. The other choices swap the radii, reverse the translations, or use diameters or squared radii instead of radii."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcbfddc2-f865-5ee0-80eb-48ff35a445b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716487} {:answer/id #uuid "ce95c532-05b9-577a-b61a-8248d55ecd17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\cos\\theta-1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716489} {:answer/id #uuid "d1300278-faf2-5f2f-afae-1cdb30cd1b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta-1,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716490} {:answer/id #uuid "d4faf6fe-ffe2-5635-8574-5e309a6be177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\cos\\theta-1,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716491} {:answer/id #uuid "d53289c6-cc58-520e-bfd8-7a5eb56d7ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta-1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716490} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29aa51d-b1ae-58c1-a7bc-d8cbca4a2b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58a5360a-9d65-58e0-8729-2af048d65c08" :question/problem "An ellipse has center $(-1,2)$, horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $2$.\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates this ellipse for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=-1$, $k=2$, $a=3$ and $b=2$. Use the radii as the coefficients, and add each coordinate of the center to the corresponding expression:\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta-1,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta+2.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal range is $[-4,2]$ and the vertical range is $[0,4]$, so these equations have the required center and radii. The other choices swap the radii, reverse the translations, use diameters, or use squared radii."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9549f84-8c31-5a20-83a2-fb07bf661de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sin\\theta-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716485} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e1ec6bb-f519-5e45-89b2-ab5abdd8cba5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716476} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba430dc8-0d79-50a8-bd32-e92c403c4331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cos\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716486} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5b42bcd-8cc9-571f-97e5-d28794a24e43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "87e16656-c93c-5144-a0cc-4c51b0c30cb2" :question/problem "![Ellipse with dashed center lines, its rightmost point A, and a counterclockwise arrow.](images/c6/c633c884fac6ead15c6ac06934ceb82280ae783aba26c136dc63f8099ee17055.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-1)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Construct parametric equations, defined for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$, that traverse the ellipse once counterclockwise, starting at $A(1,-1)$ when $\\theta=0$.\n\nUse sine or cosine of $\\theta$, without changing its argument.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe center is $(-2,-1)$ and the radii are $a=3$, $b=2$, so\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta-2,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta-1.\n$$\n\nWe must also check the starting point and direction. At $\\theta=0$, these give $(x,y)=(1,-1)=A$. At successive quarter turns they give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0 &: (1,-1),\\\\\n\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2} &: (-2,1),\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi &: (-5,-1),\\\\\n\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2} &: (-2,-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the motion is from the rightmost point to the top, then left and bottom: counterclockwise. Scaling and translating one full turn of $(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)$ traces the ellipse once; excluding $2\\pi$ avoids repeating the starting point.\n\nAs a check on the shape,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{x+2}{3}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{y+1}{2}\\right)^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fde0ba00-f53d-50c8-a9b3-3a88467f71cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716627} :answer-field/id #uuid "3863a140-c1cb-5fd9-9c5a-71a117c87aed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9524dd95-6e22-5871-b761-c0a2e7b9c533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716618}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716618} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec01a688-b8e6-5354-b054-173a9e492385" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bb946ea-6810-5bec-9c5a-297efcd4d000" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f8b73b2bed7ec7f13267d8aea0c297292fdbaa5e7f4caeab12526cff50f0f3f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $4$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $H(3,0)$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=3$ and $b=4$, so\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $H(3,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (3,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,4)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-3,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-4).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd896e0-ef79-5b9f-874f-eccfd0592f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=8\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716611} {:answer/id #uuid "64139086-28bf-5516-afab-d675b2844242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=3\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716612} {:answer/id #uuid "6b21b915-2aca-5457-8fe4-aa97d081c707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=64\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716614} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9512e6-6dc8-580a-8d8f-d07f0a5481c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716616} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8e1066-ec8e-50f9-8fc7-6ab983e9e993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=8\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716616} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e38175d-2955-5c9d-8e0c-dc1362e5632c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2f11cbe3-0991-54f4-a244-9c49ff7916c0" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7cef92b657c3af8e56c23b8c82687dcc90bcea76a161b066bc224b8fbd9f95af.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $8$ and vertical radius $3$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $A(8,0)$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=8$ and $b=3$, so\n\n$$\nx=8\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $A(8,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (8,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,3)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-8,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-3).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504d07b9-dc07-56ed-8cf2-a3bde22da4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=6\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716609} {:answer/id #uuid "b24e08c9-e2f9-5b61-86e0-fa53a5e5d3fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716620} {:answer/id #uuid "b3dde9d4-8d8c-5f51-a856-72c35c4fdc18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716621} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5df4d4-1ef0-5727-babc-0428f299f250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716622} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d62087-c468-5a97-81d3-70fed31fb8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=36\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716622} :answer-field/id #uuid "64b0fca2-0672-5c31-8953-fdee3d7b46c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c0ca723-545a-5ad3-b74e-21b02509d7f5" :question/problem "![](images/16/16655f767e57ba984b436683a914f943c9a557cd6c29250c0a365a09aec6b7f1.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $6$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $A(1,0)$?" :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=1$ and $b=6$, so\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $A(1,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (1,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,6)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-1,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-6).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c579765-6c22-5346-b2e8-b1801d12ee43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716603} :answer-field/id #uuid "28fa667e-0b54-5080-86c4-07482cc031dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c1b9f1-b9bd-5cf5-a781-1d3ff8816f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716605} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea69d93c-d897-5ef9-bb48-61208624461c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75877ff2-8677-52ce-9d76-0f7e59ad04b3" :question/problem "![](images/ac/acfd1b409c19ca2d27d84106568f3e543da599bea88e144f008adfccdd31716d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $H(3,0)$.\n\n$x=$ {{field